00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.260
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:10.080
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.890
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.600
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.390
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.450
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:21.140
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:25.050
I'm Alastair Budge, and today we are going

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.150
to be talking about the law, specifically,

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:31.940
jury trials in Britain, and their future.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:35.360
The role of jury trials is a subject of

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.630
ongoing debate, and the law might well be

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:41.910
changing, so today we're going to look at

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:45.080
the unusual idea and the uneven history

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:48.840
of the jury, and at how twelve strangers

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:51.770
with no legal training can decide

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:54.890
whether someone is guilty or not guilty.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:57.670
OK then, let's not waste a

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.210
minute and get right into it.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.450
Imagine you are charged with

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.640
a crime in England today.

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:08.530
Not a minor traffic offence.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:09.900
Something serious.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.840
Fraud, perhaps, or assault.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:15.750
You maintain that you are innocent.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:18.120
You are certain you can prove it.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:20.310
And of course, you are innocent.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:22.330
You listen to this show, which

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:23.840
means you are an upstanding,

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.130
law-abiding member of society.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:30.440
But somehow, somewhere, you've been

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:33.280
accused and charged with a crime.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.280
Your lawyer tells you to expect

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:38.870
your case to come to court

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:41.320
in approximately three years.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:42.680
Possibly four.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:46.480
Some cases, he tells you, are now

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:49.630
being scheduled to start in 2029.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.810
Until then, you'll just need to sit tight.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:55.430
The possible verdict

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:57.150
will be hanging over you.

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:59.940
It will be the first thing you think about

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:02.660
when you wake up, and the last thought you

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:05.190
have before you go to sleep every night.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:09.990
This is the reality of the Crown Court

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.490
system in England and Wales in 2026.

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:17.230
There are more than 78,000

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:19.150
cases waiting to be heard.

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.690
The backlog — the number of cases

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:25.430
sitting in this queue — has roughly

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.830
doubled since before the pandemic.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:29.910
And it is still growing.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:32.410
And let's say you are

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:34.140
the victim of a crime.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:36.380
It's the same wait, just

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:37.620
from the other side.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.170
You might have to wait two,

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:43.230
three, even four years until you

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:44.900
can have some kind of closure.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:48.470
And all that time, the person

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:51.650
accused may still be walking free.

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:55.840
Some victims, perfectly understandably,

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:59.170
wait so long for their day in court that

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:02.220
they simply give up and drop the charges.

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:05.350
The government's response, which was

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:07.710
announced earlier this year by the

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:11.480
Justice Secretary David Lammy, is radical.

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:15.770
The basic idea is to reduce the number

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:18.750
of cases going to a jury trial, and

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:22.090
to reserve jury trials more clearly

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.563
for the most serious offences.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.990
The Courts and Tribunals Bill, which is

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.300
working its way through Parliament as

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:33.830
this episode is being recorded, would

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:36.540
remove the rights of defendants to

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:39.710
elect a jury trial for what are called

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.310
“either-way offences” — things like

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:45.980
fraud, robbery, and certain drug offences.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.660
These are offences that are serious

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.187
enough to go to the Crown Court

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:55.050
but not the most serious crimes.

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:58.740
At the moment, a defendant charged with an

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:01.850
either-way offence can choose whether to

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:04.750
be tried by magistrates — who sit without

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:08.530
a jury — or by a jury in the Crown Court.

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:12.840
That choice, the government says, must go.

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:16.020
And it's worth being clear about

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:18.420
what this proposal does not include.

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:19.500
Murder.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:20.160
Rape.

00:04:20.330 --> 00:04:21.050
Terrorism.

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:22.780
Grievous bodily harm.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:25.230
Nobody in government is suggesting

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:27.220
that the most serious crimes

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:29.070
should be tried without a jury.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:32.530
The proposal is specifically about this

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.840
middle tier of offences, for now at least.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.840
Now, the legal community in Britain

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:43.200
is, to put it mildly, alarmed.

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:46.880
Lawyers, barristers, and legal academics

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:49.540
have all come out strongly against the

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:52.310
bill, with many arguing that the right to

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:55.950
a jury trial is a fundamental safeguard

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.230
against the overreach of the state.

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:01.700
It exists for a reason, they say,

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:04.490
and is one of the foundations

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:07.050
of a just and fair society.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.220
So, is it really?

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:12.350
Where does the idea of

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:14.000
jury service come from?

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:17.460
How is it used, and what are some of the

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:20.050
most famous cases of juries in action?

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:24.510
Well, let’s start with what a jury trial

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:28.780
actually is, because it might be quite an

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:32.030
unfamiliar concept, especially if you come

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:34.770
from a country with a civil-law system.

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:38.160
In England, when you are charged

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:40.287
with a serious criminal offence,

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:43.850
your guilt or innocence is decided

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:47.690
not by a judge, but by a jury.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:52.230
A jury is a group of twelve ordinary

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:54.980
citizens, chosen at random, who are

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:58.270
called to sit in court and listen to

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:01.400
the evidence presented by both sides.

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.140
At the end of the trial, they

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:06.900
retire — they leave the courtroom

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:10.120
and deliberate together in private

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:13.180
— and they return with a verdict.

00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:16.060
Guilty, or not guilty.

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:20.500
The judge's job is to oversee the

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:24.180
proceedings, to advise the jury on

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:26.850
points of law, and if the verdict

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:29.830
comes back guilty, they will decide

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:31.500
on the appropriate punishment,

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:33.950
based on the severity of the crime.

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:39.320
But the central factual question — did

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:41.010
this person commit this crime?

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:44.450
— this is answered by the twelve random

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:47.100
strangers pulled in off the street.

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:49.930
Or rather, not literally pulled

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:52.040
in off the street, but chosen at

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:54.180
random from the electoral roll.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:57.200
This is what lawyers call

00:06:57.360 --> 00:06:59.180
the adversarial system.

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:02.500
Two sides — the prosecution and

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:04.600
the defence — present their cases

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:06.630
to a neutral decision-maker.

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:10.620
It's quite different from the more

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:13.830
inquisitorial system used in France,

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.080
Germany, and most of continental

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.900
Europe, where the judge takes a far

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:21.110
more active role in investigating

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:23.250
the facts, and where ordinary

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:25.790
citizens play a much smaller part.

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:30.950
And this jury system, and the principle

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:33.860
behind it, goes back a very long way.

00:07:34.553 --> 00:07:37.433
The first jury-like systems we know of

00:07:37.743 --> 00:07:40.563
appeared in ancient Athens, around the

00:07:40.573 --> 00:07:44.033
sixth century BC. Athenian citizens

00:07:44.233 --> 00:07:46.793
could be called to serve as dikastai

00:07:47.173 --> 00:07:50.143
— citizen judges — who would hear arguments

00:07:50.333 --> 00:07:53.123
and decide cases by majority vote.

00:07:54.033 --> 00:07:56.203
Sometimes there were hundreds of

00:07:56.203 --> 00:07:58.673
them sitting on a single trial.

00:07:59.603 --> 00:08:02.053
The most famous example is the trial of

00:08:02.063 --> 00:08:06.953
Socrates in 399 BC, in which five hundred

00:08:06.953 --> 00:08:09.983
citizens voted to condemn him to death.

00:08:10.693 --> 00:08:13.163
It was, in some ways, the

00:08:13.173 --> 00:08:15.143
first great jury trial.

00:08:15.613 --> 00:08:17.573
And it produced one of history's

00:08:17.603 --> 00:08:19.273
most controversial verdicts.

00:08:19.953 --> 00:08:22.303
The English jury, however,

00:08:22.693 --> 00:08:23.933
developed much later.

00:08:24.893 --> 00:08:28.053
In etymological terms, as the keen

00:08:28.053 --> 00:08:29.593
linguists among you might have

00:08:29.663 --> 00:08:32.893
guessed, the English word "jury"

00:08:33.363 --> 00:08:35.873
comes from the Old French "juré,"

00:08:36.293 --> 00:08:39.593
meaning "sworn" — those who have taken

00:08:39.603 --> 00:08:41.813
an oath, who have made a promise.

00:08:43.103 --> 00:08:46.095
What the English did was formalise it.

00:08:46.813 --> 00:08:50.873
In 1166, King Henry II issued what

00:08:50.873 --> 00:08:53.183
is called the Assize of Clarendon.

00:08:54.003 --> 00:08:56.523
It required that in each district,

00:08:56.913 --> 00:08:59.163
twelve local men of good standing

00:08:59.403 --> 00:09:01.333
should be assembled to report

00:09:01.373 --> 00:09:03.663
on crimes and settle disputes.

00:09:04.561 --> 00:09:06.781
This was not yet the modern

00:09:06.791 --> 00:09:09.781
jury as we know it today, but it

00:09:09.781 --> 00:09:12.241
established the principle: twelve

00:09:12.261 --> 00:09:14.441
local people, deciding together.

00:09:15.581 --> 00:09:19.181
Then, in 1215, came Magna

00:09:19.181 --> 00:09:22.021
Carta — the great document forced

00:09:22.041 --> 00:09:24.081
on King John by his barons.

00:09:24.971 --> 00:09:27.101
It contains a clause that has

00:09:27.101 --> 00:09:30.851
shaped the law ever since: "No free

00:09:30.851 --> 00:09:33.771
man shall be seized, imprisoned,

00:09:34.021 --> 00:09:36.701
or dispossessed, except by the

00:09:36.711 --> 00:09:39.211
lawful judgement of his equals."

00:09:40.041 --> 00:09:42.491
The important idea there is not the

00:09:42.521 --> 00:09:46.391
exact modern mechanism, but the principle

00:09:46.411 --> 00:09:49.781
behind it: that justice should not simply

00:09:49.781 --> 00:09:52.181
be the arbitrary will of the ruler.

00:09:52.821 --> 00:09:54.401
The lawful judgement of "his

00:09:54.781 --> 00:09:56.921
equals" — people like him.

00:09:57.401 --> 00:09:59.771
Peers judging peers.

00:10:00.701 --> 00:10:03.671
So when the British government talks

00:10:03.671 --> 00:10:06.301
about removing jury trials for a

00:10:06.311 --> 00:10:08.861
large category of offences, it's not

00:10:09.331 --> 00:10:12.561
proposing a minor administrative tweak.

00:10:12.881 --> 00:10:15.701
It's proposing to dismantle a mechanism

00:10:15.741 --> 00:10:18.511
of justice that has existed, in some

00:10:18.511 --> 00:10:20.941
form, for over eight hundred years.

00:10:21.911 --> 00:10:24.961
Now, if you live in a country where the

00:10:24.971 --> 00:10:28.051
legal system doesn't use juries, you might

00:10:28.051 --> 00:10:30.341
be thinking: why would you need them?

00:10:31.581 --> 00:10:33.301
It's a perfectly reasonable question

00:10:33.301 --> 00:10:36.846
to ask: why use ordinary people at all?

00:10:37.276 --> 00:10:39.841
Why not have a judge decide every case?

00:10:40.681 --> 00:10:42.161
After all, they are a

00:10:42.191 --> 00:10:44.181
professional, trained in the law,

00:10:44.441 --> 00:10:46.751
experienced in weighing evidence.

00:10:47.511 --> 00:10:49.721
The answer is about power.

00:10:50.701 --> 00:10:53.351
A judge is appointed by the state.

00:10:53.651 --> 00:10:55.521
They work within the system.

00:10:55.821 --> 00:10:58.181
They have a career to protect and

00:10:58.181 --> 00:11:00.511
professional instincts shaped by

00:11:00.521 --> 00:11:03.571
years inside the legal establishment.

00:11:04.661 --> 00:11:07.121
A jury is none of those things.

00:11:07.611 --> 00:11:09.931
It is twelve people chosen at

00:11:09.931 --> 00:11:12.581
random, with no career to protect,

00:11:12.871 --> 00:11:15.651
no deference to authority, and

00:11:15.681 --> 00:11:17.701
no obligation to the government.

00:11:18.721 --> 00:11:21.211
That randomness is the point.

00:11:21.761 --> 00:11:24.501
You cannot bribe, pressurise or

00:11:24.521 --> 00:11:26.701
intimidate twelve strangers before

00:11:26.701 --> 00:11:28.391
they walk into the room, because

00:11:28.641 --> 00:11:29.481
you do not know who they are.

00:11:30.801 --> 00:11:32.641
A single judge, however,

00:11:33.131 --> 00:11:34.921
is a known quantity.

00:11:35.771 --> 00:11:37.561
If a government wants to

00:11:37.561 --> 00:11:40.331
convict someone, a judge is a

00:11:40.341 --> 00:11:42.791
far easier target than a jury.

00:11:44.231 --> 00:11:45.871
There is also something

00:11:45.871 --> 00:11:47.661
important about legitimacy.

00:11:48.921 --> 00:11:52.851
When a jury convicts someone, the wider

00:11:52.851 --> 00:11:55.941
community can see that the verdict came

00:11:55.941 --> 00:11:58.591
from ordinary people — people like them.

00:11:58.991 --> 00:12:00.701
It was not handed down

00:12:00.951 --> 00:12:02.231
by a distant official.

00:12:03.221 --> 00:12:05.981
Yes, it can be slow and frustrating,

00:12:06.611 --> 00:12:09.751
but for the most part, it works.

00:12:10.411 --> 00:12:12.891
Twelve people need to be sufficiently

00:12:12.921 --> 00:12:15.081
convinced, based on the evidence

00:12:15.261 --> 00:12:16.981
they have seen, that someone

00:12:17.211 --> 00:12:19.801
is either guilty or not guilty.

00:12:21.101 --> 00:12:23.721
Sometimes, however, a jury

00:12:24.171 --> 00:12:25.624
does something unexpected.

00:12:26.467 --> 00:12:29.351
It acquits someone who is, by any

00:12:29.451 --> 00:12:32.101
technical reading of the law, guilty.

00:12:32.881 --> 00:12:36.241
Lawyers call this jury nullification.

00:12:37.321 --> 00:12:40.431
It is the power of a jury to refuse

00:12:40.431 --> 00:12:43.751
to convict even when the facts seem

00:12:43.751 --> 00:12:46.561
clear, because the jurors believe that

00:12:46.611 --> 00:12:50.361
convicting would produce an unjust result.

00:12:51.561 --> 00:12:53.241
The law says he did it;

00:12:53.911 --> 00:12:55.601
we say it doesn't matter.

00:12:56.381 --> 00:12:58.151
We'll come to a few examples

00:12:58.181 --> 00:12:59.111
of this in a minute.

00:12:59.781 --> 00:13:02.981
Now, the most famous jury trial

00:13:03.031 --> 00:13:05.501
in living memory is probably

00:13:05.521 --> 00:13:08.681
the OJ Simpson case, in 1995.

00:13:09.761 --> 00:13:11.711
You might know this story already,

00:13:12.071 --> 00:13:14.281
and if you're a member, you might

00:13:14.291 --> 00:13:17.381
remember it from episode number 409.

00:13:18.481 --> 00:13:20.971
But as a quick reminder, the former

00:13:21.031 --> 00:13:23.787
American football star was acquitted

00:13:24.057 --> 00:13:26.221
of murdering his ex-wife, who was

00:13:26.361 --> 00:13:29.601
found not guilty, despite overwhelming

00:13:29.631 --> 00:13:31.181
physical evidence against him.

00:13:32.551 --> 00:13:36.331
Now, rather than revisit that case, let

00:13:36.331 --> 00:13:39.831
me tell you about a few lesser-known cases

00:13:40.161 --> 00:13:42.871
— because they show something more specific

00:13:43.051 --> 00:13:45.181
about how the system actually works.

00:13:46.171 --> 00:13:50.141
The first dates from 1670, when

00:13:50.141 --> 00:13:52.441
the principle of jury nullification

00:13:52.451 --> 00:13:53.651
was established in England.

00:13:54.591 --> 00:13:57.581
This is the case of William Penn,

00:13:57.961 --> 00:14:00.151
the man who would later found the

00:14:00.171 --> 00:14:03.271
colony of Pennsylvania in America.

00:14:03.941 --> 00:14:06.931
Now, Penn was a Quaker preacher.

00:14:07.501 --> 00:14:10.041
He was accused of holding what was called

00:14:10.071 --> 00:14:13.501
an unlawful religious assembly — in

00:14:13.501 --> 00:14:16.501
other words, preaching publicly in a way

00:14:16.501 --> 00:14:18.841
that the authorities said was illegal.

00:14:19.901 --> 00:14:23.291
Penn was clearly guilty under the law.

00:14:24.061 --> 00:14:27.281
But the jury refused to convict him.

00:14:28.101 --> 00:14:29.861
The judge was furious.

00:14:30.201 --> 00:14:33.051
He locked the jurors up without food

00:14:33.061 --> 00:14:35.901
or drink to force a guilty verdict.

00:14:36.531 --> 00:14:37.521
They still refused.

00:14:38.141 --> 00:14:40.511
A higher court eventually ruled that

00:14:40.511 --> 00:14:42.951
a jury could not be punished for its

00:14:42.951 --> 00:14:45.351
verdict, whatever that verdict might be.

00:14:46.411 --> 00:14:48.331
And that principle has

00:14:48.331 --> 00:14:49.871
remained ever since.

00:14:51.061 --> 00:14:54.131
Now, fast-forward three centuries.

00:14:54.471 --> 00:14:58.431
It is February 1985, and a man named

00:14:58.551 --> 00:15:01.191
Clive Ponting is sitting in the

00:15:01.211 --> 00:15:04.001
dock at the Old Bailey — London's

00:15:04.031 --> 00:15:05.921
most famous criminal court.

00:15:07.251 --> 00:15:10.351
Ponting is a senior civil servant

00:15:10.551 --> 00:15:12.531
at the Ministry of Defence.

00:15:13.251 --> 00:15:16.321
He has leaked classified documents to a

00:15:16.331 --> 00:15:18.971
Labour Member of Parliament — documents

00:15:19.171 --> 00:15:21.731
that revealed the Argentine Navy ship

00:15:22.241 --> 00:15:24.771
General Belgrano had been sailing

00:15:25.151 --> 00:15:28.011
away from the British exclusion zone

00:15:28.341 --> 00:15:30.361
when it was sunk during the Falklands

00:15:30.361 --> 00:15:35.401
War, killing 323 Argentine sailors.

00:15:36.591 --> 00:15:38.651
Importantly, the Conservative

00:15:38.691 --> 00:15:40.851
government had told Parliament

00:15:41.241 --> 00:15:42.931
something quite different.

00:15:44.331 --> 00:15:46.631
Ponting is charged under

00:15:46.631 --> 00:15:48.341
the Official Secrets Act.

00:15:49.041 --> 00:15:50.981
He is, on the face of

00:15:50.981 --> 00:15:52.791
it, obviously guilty.

00:15:53.221 --> 00:15:55.541
He admits leaking the documents.

00:15:56.181 --> 00:15:59.191
The judge, summing up, tells the jury

00:15:59.191 --> 00:16:01.371
that "the interests of the state"

00:16:01.881 --> 00:16:03.831
are the same as the interests of the

00:16:03.831 --> 00:16:06.781
government, and directs them accordingly.

00:16:08.421 --> 00:16:10.391
The jury retires.

00:16:10.941 --> 00:16:12.711
It comes back with a

00:16:12.741 --> 00:16:15.471
verdict of not guilty.

00:16:16.721 --> 00:16:18.851
The government is furious.

00:16:19.391 --> 00:16:22.301
But the jury had evidently decided

00:16:22.571 --> 00:16:24.681
that telling Parliament the truth

00:16:24.981 --> 00:16:28.211
about the deaths of 323 people

00:16:28.471 --> 00:16:30.811
was not a crime worth punishing.

00:16:32.211 --> 00:16:33.711
And this theme has

00:16:33.711 --> 00:16:35.561
continued to the modern day.

00:16:36.931 --> 00:16:40.301
In January 2022, four defendants

00:16:40.411 --> 00:16:43.531
appear before a jury at Bristol Crown

00:16:43.531 --> 00:16:46.691
Court, charged with criminal damage.

00:16:47.411 --> 00:16:50.251
They had pulled down the statue of Edward

00:16:50.311 --> 00:16:52.811
Colston, an eighteenth-century slave

00:16:52.831 --> 00:16:55.111
trader, during the Black Lives Matter

00:16:55.111 --> 00:16:58.691
protests of June 2020, and they had

00:16:58.691 --> 00:17:01.641
thrown the statue  into Bristol harbour.

00:17:02.811 --> 00:17:05.111
They do not deny doing it.

00:17:05.111 --> 00:17:06.761
The footage exists.

00:17:07.771 --> 00:17:10.271
The jury finds them not

00:17:10.351 --> 00:17:12.921
guilty, by eleven votes to one.

00:17:14.451 --> 00:17:17.771
The then Justice Secretary was furious.

00:17:18.061 --> 00:17:20.541
And so were many members of the public.

00:17:21.731 --> 00:17:24.051
Others pointed out that the jury

00:17:24.421 --> 00:17:26.031
had done exactly what juries are

00:17:26.031 --> 00:17:28.841
meant to do: reach a verdict the

00:17:28.841 --> 00:17:31.921
jurors considered just, based on

00:17:31.921 --> 00:17:34.191
the full circumstances of the case.

00:17:35.721 --> 00:17:38.521
Critics, however, argued that this

00:17:38.521 --> 00:17:41.161
looked uncomfortably close to jurors

00:17:41.621 --> 00:17:43.771
overriding the law because they

00:17:43.771 --> 00:17:45.821
sympathised with the defendants.

00:17:47.261 --> 00:17:50.331
This sparked a fierce debate over

00:17:50.331 --> 00:17:53.241
the role of juries: should juries be

00:17:53.241 --> 00:17:56.231
allowed, in effect, to rewrite the

00:17:56.241 --> 00:17:58.981
law through their verdicts, or is

00:17:58.991 --> 00:18:02.181
that unpredictability precisely part

00:18:02.181 --> 00:18:03.661
of their constitutional function?

00:18:04.921 --> 00:18:07.391
Now, you may have strong views on

00:18:07.391 --> 00:18:10.401
this, but what is clear is that these

00:18:10.401 --> 00:18:13.241
cases make an argument for juries

00:18:13.491 --> 00:18:16.161
that no efficiency review ever will.

00:18:17.051 --> 00:18:18.921
The government couldn't predict

00:18:19.061 --> 00:18:21.111
how any of those juries would vote.

00:18:21.651 --> 00:18:25.331
That uncertainty — that independence

00:18:25.341 --> 00:18:27.671
from the state — that is exactly

00:18:27.791 --> 00:18:30.091
what the bill would start to erode.

00:18:30.811 --> 00:18:33.411
No longer would twelve strangers

00:18:33.661 --> 00:18:35.361
be responsible for deciding

00:18:35.511 --> 00:18:36.941
whether someone is guilty.

00:18:37.571 --> 00:18:39.861
That responsibility would instead

00:18:40.201 --> 00:18:42.411
fall to magistrates or a judge.

00:18:43.521 --> 00:18:46.181
Now, the government is not necessarily

00:18:46.451 --> 00:18:48.101
saying that this is a better

00:18:48.101 --> 00:18:50.811
system in terms of fairness; its

00:18:50.811 --> 00:18:53.271
argument is primarily practical.

00:18:54.001 --> 00:18:56.761
The backlog is over 78,000

00:18:56.781 --> 00:18:59.511
cases and growing, with official

00:18:59.511 --> 00:19:01.311
modelling suggesting it could

00:19:01.311 --> 00:19:05.161
rise to 200,000 by 2035.

00:19:06.201 --> 00:19:08.801
Victims are waiting years.

00:19:09.381 --> 00:19:12.461
Fewer jury trials, so the argument goes,

00:19:12.761 --> 00:19:16.291
will be faster and cheaper, and will allow

00:19:16.301 --> 00:19:19.491
the most serious cases to receive jury

00:19:19.491 --> 00:19:22.371
trials without the same years-long wait.

00:19:23.301 --> 00:19:26.311
But the counterargument is powerful.

00:19:27.221 --> 00:19:31.201
The value of the jury lies precisely in

00:19:31.201 --> 00:19:35.151
its awkwardness — the fact that it's slow,

00:19:35.231 --> 00:19:37.741
unpredictable, and answerable to no one.

00:19:38.431 --> 00:19:41.311
A state that can imprison its citizens

00:19:41.651 --> 00:19:44.091
without needing to persuade ordinary

00:19:44.091 --> 00:19:46.571
members of the public is a state with

00:19:46.601 --> 00:19:48.901
considerably more concentrated power.

00:19:50.061 --> 00:19:51.571
And then there is the

00:19:51.601 --> 00:19:53.251
slippery slope argument.

00:19:54.151 --> 00:19:56.911
The current proposal is careful to

00:19:56.941 --> 00:19:59.681
exclude the most serious crimes.

00:20:00.471 --> 00:20:03.481
But the critics' concern is not really

00:20:03.481 --> 00:20:05.841
about where the line is drawn today.

00:20:06.531 --> 00:20:09.341
It's about what happens once you accept

00:20:09.501 --> 00:20:11.361
that the line can be moved at all.

00:20:12.341 --> 00:20:14.941
Once you establish that efficiency

00:20:15.161 --> 00:20:17.271
can override eight hundred years

00:20:17.441 --> 00:20:19.071
of constitutional principle,

00:20:19.581 --> 00:20:21.621
the question is: how far?

00:20:22.481 --> 00:20:25.001
A government that reduces jury rights

00:20:25.011 --> 00:20:27.181
for fraud and robbery has already

00:20:27.181 --> 00:20:28.691
won the argument in principle.

00:20:29.321 --> 00:20:31.041
What stops a future government

00:20:31.281 --> 00:20:33.501
from extending that logic to

00:20:33.501 --> 00:20:36.171
other offences, if a new backlog

00:20:36.191 --> 00:20:38.821
develops, or a new pressure emerges?

00:20:39.837 --> 00:20:43.687
A poll in 2025 found that fifty-four per

00:20:43.687 --> 00:20:46.677
cent of people in Britain would prefer to

00:20:46.677 --> 00:20:49.877
be tried by a jury if accused of a crime.

00:20:50.797 --> 00:20:53.527
Even knowing all the delays and

00:20:53.737 --> 00:20:56.167
imperfections, a majority of the

00:20:56.167 --> 00:20:59.367
population trusts twelve strangers

00:20:59.597 --> 00:21:01.857
more than it trusts a single judge.

00:21:02.637 --> 00:21:05.477
And that, perhaps, is the most important

00:21:05.487 --> 00:21:07.507
thing to understand about jury service.

00:21:08.107 --> 00:21:09.867
It is not simply a mechanism

00:21:10.217 --> 00:21:12.107
for producing verdicts.

00:21:12.337 --> 00:21:15.347
It is an expression of a belief — the

00:21:15.357 --> 00:21:18.407
belief that ordinary citizens, and not

00:21:18.457 --> 00:21:21.327
the state, should have the final say over

00:21:21.327 --> 00:21:23.777
whether one of their number is imprisoned.

00:21:25.037 --> 00:21:27.277
Britain is debating whether to

00:21:27.277 --> 00:21:30.287
give that up, and, in so doing, is

00:21:30.357 --> 00:21:32.797
asking whether eight centuries of

00:21:32.817 --> 00:21:35.117
constitutional principle are really

00:21:35.117 --> 00:21:37.627
worth sacrificing for the sake of

00:21:37.627 --> 00:21:40.247
clearing an administrative backlog.

00:21:42.027 --> 00:21:43.837
OK then, that is it for

00:21:43.837 --> 00:21:46.657
today's episode on jury trials.

00:21:46.717 --> 00:21:48.057
I hope it's been an interesting one

00:21:48.327 --> 00:21:49.967
and that you've learnt something new.

00:21:50.427 --> 00:21:52.057
As always, I would love to know

00:21:52.057 --> 00:21:53.497
what you thought of this episode.

00:21:53.817 --> 00:21:56.147
Do juries exist in your country?

00:21:56.667 --> 00:21:58.707
If so, how do they work?

00:21:58.997 --> 00:22:00.257
And are there similar

00:22:00.257 --> 00:22:01.667
proposals to change them?

00:22:02.267 --> 00:22:04.007
Let me know in the comments below,

00:22:04.247 --> 00:22:05.597
if you're listening to this somewhere

00:22:05.597 --> 00:22:07.537
where you can comment, and for the

00:22:07.537 --> 00:22:09.237
members among you, you can head right

00:22:09.247 --> 00:22:11.307
into our community forum, which is at

00:22:11.307 --> 00:22:13.907
community.leonardoenglish.com and get

00:22:13.907 --> 00:22:15.927
chatting away to other curious minds.

00:22:16.517 --> 00:22:18.337
You've been listening to English Learning

00:22:18.337 --> 00:22:20.967
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:22:21.307 --> 00:22:24.077
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:22:24.077 --> 00:22:26.267
I'll catch you in the next episode.

